A||||10||racefan||Ignition switch sticks||||1||
Z||000000||racefan||12-17-2001||08:06 PM||||I have a problem where my ignition switch sticks when starting. Sometimes I have to turn it back counter-clockwise to keep the starter from constantly cranking. The key goes in and out okay, but the tumbler sticks. Can I use something like WD40 to free it up? Or, are there other lubricants to use? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||12-17-2001||08:13 PM||||This can be caused in several places inside the column. You can try a little wd40 or something in the key hole but don't count on it working.<P>It could be<BR>worn lock<BR>worn switch<BR>worn gears inside column<BR>could just need clean and lube inside column.<P>None of them are much fun to fix.<P>there's notes for column stuff in my cave.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||lawrence||12-17-2001||08:46 PM||||It wouldn't hurt to try WD40.  However, I never could stand the smell of that stuff.  You might first try some dry graphite.  If that doesn't help, some silicone spray might do the trick.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe/products" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe/products</A>  - WebElectric Products||||reg||1
Z||000003||GTDude||12-17-2001||11:57 PM||||From experience it's probably the lock cyl itself.  Can you remove the key with the ignition switch in the on position?  Does the key look original and all worn out.  In either of these situations you need to replace the lock cylinder before you go someplace and the tumblers fall and the key won't turn the switch.  Happens all the time.  Good luck....it should cost less than $100 even at the dealership.  I usually do them for about $50.00<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||V8Dreamer||12-18-2001||09:51 AM||||IMO it would probably be the lock cylinder....my GT left me at Wal-Mart about a month ago when mine broke....I think it was about $20 plus the $20 for the lock-plate removing tool from Autozone (they will give you your $20 back if you return the tool)  With the help of Rockcrawl (ie, I watched and handed him my tools) we (he) had it fixed in about 15 minutes despite the girls walking by whispering "oh my god, I think they're stealing that car..."<P>-Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000005||racefan||12-18-2001||12:49 PM||||How do I get the old tumbler out? Do I need to remove the steering wheel or can this be done with it on?||||reg||5
Z||000006||tesmith66||12-18-2001||01:24 PM||||The wheel has to come off.<P>------------------<BR>Timothy E. Smith<BR>1986 SE V6<BR>1984 Coupe||||reg||9
Z||000007||fieroman_5000||12-18-2001||04:23 PM||||Well, to remove the tumbler, first you need to purchase a steering wheel and lockplate removal tool. Then remove the steering wheel (there is usually instructions on this in the package)You will see once the steering wheel is removed there is this black plastic cover thingy that come off pretty easy (just pry it off with a phillips or something) Then you will see a steel metal disc...this is the lock plate. Now using the lock plate removal tool (and following the instrucions) gently remove the lock plate. Then you will be able to see the lock cylinder. By removing the one screw on the bottom side of it you can pull it out of the steering column. Insert the replacement and reverse the process for finishing up. I did this on my '84...it might be differant on yours. Hope this helps!<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000008||fieroman_5000||12-18-2001||04:26 PM||||Oh...and one more thing...WELCOME TO THE FORUM!!!<P>    <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/jump.gif"> <P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||GTDude||12-18-2001||10:55 PM||||If you get the tool.....the hardest thing about the lock plate is the little clip that holds it in position.  Can be hard to get off. I use an ice pic and a small screwdriver.  Works well once you the get the technique down. Then all you do is remove the big spring and round horn button.  Then turn the ign cyl to the on position<BR>the is a #15 torx screw directly under the cyl. on the inside. Remove that, then the little plastic and brass square piece with two metal arms out.  Pull the old cyl out...stick the new one in...and reverse the procedure.  Should prob take you about an hour since you've never done it before.  Easy job.  Good Luck.<P>Phil Pettus<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_1.JPG"> <P>------------------<BR>GTDude's 86GT||||reg||1
Z||000010||fieroman_5000||12-19-2001||08:36 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GTDude:<BR><B>If you get the tool.....the hardest thing about the lock plate is the little clip that holds it in position.  Can be hard to get off. I use an ice pic and a small screwdriver.  Works well once you the get the technique down. Then all you do is remove the big spring and round horn button.  Then turn the ign cyl to the on position<BR>the is a #15 torx screw directly under the cyl. on the inside. Remove that, then the little plastic and brass square piece with two metal arms out.  Pull the old cyl out...stick the new one in...and reverse the procedure.  Should prob take you about an hour since you've never done it before.  Easy job.  Good Luck.<P>Phil Pettus<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSCF0003a_1.JPG">  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sorry I forgot the details.<P>Fieroman <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
